      id: "r01", date: "2026-09-25", theme: "人与自然", level: "四级",
      title: "Why Our Cities Need More Trees",
      text: "When we think about city life, we usually picture tall buildings, busy roads and bright advertisements. Trees, by contrast, seem like decoration — pleasant, but not essential. A growing number of urban planners argue that this view is seriously mistaken. According to their research, trees perform work that no machine can easily replace. On a hot summer afternoon, a street lined with mature trees can be several degrees cooler than a bare one, because leaves block sunlight and release water into the air. This cooling effect reduces the demand for air conditioning in nearby buildings and therefore saves energy. Trees also clean the air in a simple, physical way: their leaves catch dust and other tiny particles that would otherwise enter our lungs. In addition, they absorb the noise of traffic, which improves daily life for people who live beside main roads. Mental health researchers add another benefit. Studies of hospital patients and office workers suggest that even a short view of green leaves can lower stress and help people concentrate. Some cities have responded by planting trees along walking routes and turning unused land into small parks. Citizens can help as well, for example by caring for the trees near their homes and reporting damaged ones to the local government. Of course, trees cannot solve every environmental problem, and planting them requires long-term care. Yet compared with many expensive technical solutions, they offer remarkable value at a very low cost. Perhaps the lesson is simple: the best city is not the one with the most machines, but the one that remembers it is built for people — and for the nature that keeps people well.",
      trans: "想到城市生活，我们通常浮现的是高楼、繁忙的道路和明亮的广告。相比之下，树木似乎只是装饰——让人愉悦，却并非必需。越来越多城市规划者认为，这种看法大错特错。根据他们的研究，树木完成着机器难以替代的工作。炎热夏日的下午，种满成年树木的街道能比光秃的街道凉爽数度，因为树叶遮挡阳光并向空气中释放水分。这种降温效果降低了周边建筑的空调需求，从而节省能源。树木还以简单物理的方式净化空气：叶片截留尘埃和其他微小颗粒，否则它们会进入我们的肺。此外，树木吸收交通噪音，改善了住在主干道旁人们的日常生活。心理健康研究者还指出另一项好处：对住院病人和办公室职员的研究表明，即使短暂看到绿叶也能降低压力、帮助集中注意力。一些城市已通过在步行道沿线种树、把闲置土地改造成小公园来回应。市民同样可以出力，例如照料家附近的树木，向地方政府报告受损的树。当然，树木无法解决所有环境问题，种下它们也需要长期养护。但与许多昂贵的技术方案相比，它们以极低成本提供了可观价值。也许道理很简单：最好的城市不是机械最多的城市，而是记得自己为人而建——也为守护人的自然而建。",
