      id: "r14", date: "2026-09-25", theme: "科技", level: "四级",
      title: "Paper Books and Screens: A Reading Habit in Transition",
      text: "For hundreds of years, reading meant paper. Today, a large share of what students read arrives on a screen, and the change has produced a quiet debate about whether the medium affects the mind. Supporters of digital reading point to obvious practical advantages. A single device can hold thousands of books, search for a word instantly, adjust the size of the text, and carry a dictionary inside. For students who study in crowded dormitories, the convenience is not a small matter. Researchers, however, have noticed an interesting pattern. In several experiments, readers who used paper remembered the order of events in a story better, and were more accurate when asked where a particular piece of information appeared. One explanation is physical: paper gives the eyes and hands stable landmarks such as page numbers and the thickness of the remaining pages, which help the brain build a mental map. Screens provide fewer landmarks because the text scrolls and shifts. None of this means that digital reading is harmful. It seems to suit different purposes: scanning news, searching information and reading short articles are usually faster on a screen. Deep study of a difficult text, however, may still benefit from paper, or at least from habits that imitate it, such as taking notes by hand. Teachers now suggest a mixed approach. Read widely on whatever is convenient, they advise, but for material that must be remembered precisely, choose the form that forces slower attention. In the end, the question is less about which medium is superior and more about whether students understand the strengths of each.",
      trans: "几百年来，阅读意味着纸张。如今，学生读的内容有很大一部分来自屏幕，这一变化引发了关于媒介是否影响心智的温和争论。数字阅读的支持者指出明显的实用优势：一台设备能装下数千本书、瞬时查词、调整字号，还内置词典。对在拥挤宿舍学习的学生来说，这不是小事。然而研究者注意到一个有趣的规律：在若干实验中，用纸阅读的人对故事中事件的顺序记得更清楚，在被问及某条信息出现的位置时也更准确。一种解释是物理性的：纸给眼睛和手提供了稳定的路标，如页码和剩余书页的厚度，帮助大脑建立心理地图；屏幕则因文字滚动变化而路标更少。这并不意味着数字阅读有害。它似乎适合不同的目的：浏览新闻、检索信息和阅读短文在屏幕上通常更快；但研读艰深文本仍可能受益于纸张，或至少受益于模仿纸张的习惯，比如手写笔记。教师现在建议混合方式：随时方便地广泛阅读，但对于必须精确记忆的材料，选择迫使你放慢注意力的形式。归根结底，问题不在于哪种媒介更优，而在于学生是否理解各自的长处。",
