      id: "r07", date: "2026-09-25", theme: "传统文化", level: "四级",
      title: "The Mid-Autumn Festival: A Night of Moon and Reunion",
      text: "The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month, when the moon is believed to reach its fullest and brightest point of the year. Together with the Spring Festival and the Dragon Boat Festival, it is counted among the most important traditional holidays in China. In 2006, the festival was officially added to the national list of intangible cultural heritage, a step that reflected growing public awareness of the value of traditional culture. The origins of the festival can be traced back more than three thousand years to ancient ceremonies in which people offered sacrifices to the moon. By the Tang Dynasty, admiring the bright full moon had become a popular custom among poets and scholars, and many of the finest classical poems were written on such nights. Su Shi, a famous poet of the Song Dynasty, expressed the feeling of millions in a single line: may we all be blessed with long life, and share the beauty of the moon even thousands of miles apart. For ordinary families, however, the festival is above all a matter of reunion. Family members travel long distances to gather for dinner, share mooncakes, drink osmanthus wine, and talk under the moonlight. The round shape of the mooncake carries a special meaning: it symbolizes completeness and family unity. Different regions of China have developed their own styles, from the sweet lotus-seed paste of the south to the mixed nuts of the north, and arguing about which tastes best has become a gentle national tradition in itself. In modern society, people often live far away from their hometowns for study or work. Yet technology has given the old festival new forms: video calls allow families to admire the same moon together on a screen, and express delivery carries mooncakes across the country within days. Cultural experts point out that these changes do not weaken the festival. On the contrary, they prove that a living tradition is one that continues to grow while keeping its heart unchanged.",
      trans: "中秋节在农历八月十五，这一天月亮被认为达到一年中最圆最亮。与春节、端午节一起，它是中国最重要的传统节日之一。2006 年，中秋节被正式列入国家级非物质文化遗产名录，这一举措体现了公众对传统文化价值的日益重视。节日起源可追溯到三千多年前人们祭月的古老仪式。到唐代，赏月已在诗人文士中蔚然成风，许多最优秀的古典诗篇都写于这样的夜晚。宋代著名诗人苏轼用一句话道出了千万人的心声：但愿人长久，千里共婵娟。而对普通家庭来说，这个节日首先是团圆。家人长途跋涉团聚吃晚饭、分月饼、饮桂花酒、月下畅谈。月饼的圆形象征圆满与家庭和睦。中国各地形成了各自的月饼风格，从南方香甜的莲蓉到北方的五仁，争论哪种最好吃本身也成了全国性的温和传统。现代社会中，人们常因学习工作远离家乡。但科技赋予古老节日新的形式：视频通话让家人隔着屏幕共赏同一轮明月，快递几天内就能把月饼送到全国。文化专家指出，这些变化并未削弱节日，相反，它们证明一个活着的传统，就是在保持内核不变的同时继续生长。",
