      id: "r08", date: "2026-09-25", theme: "环境保护", level: "四级",
      title: "Small Actions, Big Impact: How Campuses Go Green",
      text: "In recent years, environmental protection has moved from a distant slogan to a daily practice on many university campuses. Students are encouraged to sort their rubbish into different bins, bring their own cups to coffee shops, and print on both sides of the paper. Some dormitories have started a simple system in which students exchange used textbooks at the end of each semester instead of throwing them away. Supporters say that such activities are valuable not because a single action changes the world, but because repeated small habits gradually shape the way people think. A student volunteer explained the idea in a practical way: when you sort rubbish for a month, you begin to notice how much packaging you use, and that awareness naturally leads to more careful choices. Universities have also introduced courses on sustainable development, inviting experts to discuss topics such as energy saving, clean water and green transport. Critics sometimes argue that individual efforts are too small to matter when large factories produce far more pollution. Environmental teachers respond that both levels are necessary. Laws and technology can change industry, they say, while personal habits create the social pressure that makes such laws possible. For college students, the message is encouraging: you do not need to wait until you have power to protect the environment. You only need to begin with the small choices that are already within your reach.",
      trans: "近年来，环境保护在许多大学校园里已从遥远的口号变成日常实践。学生们被鼓励把垃圾分类投入不同的桶、自带杯子去咖啡店、双面打印纸张。一些宿舍还开始了简单做法：每学期末学生互相交换旧教材，而不是扔掉。支持者认为，这些活动的价值不在于一个动作能改变世界，而在于重复的小习惯会逐渐塑造人的思维方式。一位学生志愿者用很实际的方式解释：当你坚持分类一个月，就会开始注意到自己用了多少包装，而这种意识自然会带来更谨慎的选择。高校还开设了可持续发展课程，邀请专家讨论节能、清洁水和绿色出行等话题。也有批评者认为，当大工厂产生多得多的污染时，个人努力太渺小。环境课老师回应说，两个层面都必不可少：法律和技术能改变工业，而个人习惯会形成推动法律出台的社会压力。对大学生的启示是令人鼓舞的：不必等到握有权力才去保护环境，只要从自己力所能及的小选择开始。",
