      id: "r17", date: "2026-09-25", theme: "仿真练习", level: "四级",
      title: "The Value of a Part-time Job on Campus",
      text: "Many students take part-time jobs during their college years, and opinions about the practice differ sharply. Some teachers worry that work will take time away from study, while parents often support it as a way of learning responsibility. A closer look at the experience suggests that both views contain part of the truth. The benefits are not mainly financial. Students who work a few hours a week report that they plan their time more carefully, because they can no longer assume that an evening is always free. They also learn to communicate with people who are not classmates or teachers, from customers who complain to managers who give instructions. Such experience is valuable when they later apply for jobs, since interviewers are usually more interested in what a candidate has actually done than in what a candidate claims to believe. The risks, however, are real. Research indicates that academic performance begins to suffer when students work more than about fifteen hours a week, especially during examination periods. There is also the question of quality. A job that involves standing behind a counter for long hours teaches patience, but it may not develop any skill that a student hopes to use in the future. Advisers therefore suggest a simple test before accepting an offer: ask whether the work will teach something, and whether the schedule can be adjusted in busy weeks. If the answer to either question is no, the cost may be higher than the pay. Seen this way, a part-time job is neither automatically good nor automatically harmful. It is a tool, and like any tool, its value depends on how carefully it is chosen and used.",
      trans: "许多学生在大学期间做兼职，对此看法分歧很大。有些老师担心工作会挤占学习时间，而父母常常支持它作为学习责任感的途径。细看这段经历，会发现两种观点都各有道理。其益处主要不在金钱。每周工作几小时的学生反映，他们更仔细地规划时间，因为再也不能假设晚上总是空闲的。他们还学会与并非同学或老师的人沟通，从抱怨的顾客到发出指令的主管。这种经验在日后求职时很有价值，因为面试官通常更关心应聘者真正做过什么，而不是他声称相信什么。然而风险也实实在在。研究表明，当学生每周工作超过约十五小时，学业表现就会开始受损，考试期间尤其明显。还有质量问题：一份长时间站在柜台后的工作能教人耐心，但可能无法培养学生在未来希望使用的任何技能。因此指导老师建议在接受工作前做一个简单测试：问这份工作能否教会你什么，以及忙碌的周次能否调整排班。如果两个问题的答案都是否，代价可能高于报酬。这样看来，兼职本身既不是天然有益的，也不是天然有害的。它是一种工具，像任何工具一样，其价值取决于选择和使用得是否慎重。",
