      id: "r27", date: "2026-09-25", theme: "科技", level: "六级",
      title: "Autonomous Vehicles: Who Is Responsible When No One Drives?",
      text: "The technical debate about self-driving cars has always been easier than the legal one. Engineers can argue about sensor ranges and reaction times; lawyers must decide what happens when a vehicle makes a decision that harms someone. That question becomes sharper as automation increases. A car with a human driver who must remain attentive is legally straightforward: the driver is responsible. A car that requires no attention at all transfers responsibility to the manufacturer, yet not entirely, since an owner who neglects maintenance may still be at fault. Between those poles lies the most difficult category, partial automation, in which the system drives and the human is expected to intervene within seconds when asked. Human factors research suggests this arrangement may be the most dangerous of all, because attention decays quickly when a task seems unnecessary. Drivers who have supervised an automated system for twenty minutes respond to sudden demands far more slowly than they believe they would. Regulators have responded unevenly. Some jurisdictions permit extensive testing with a safety driver present; others allow fully driverless operation in defined areas, where speeds are low and routes are mapped. Insurers, meanwhile, are quietly rewriting their assumptions, because the frequency of minor collisions may fall while the cost of a rare software-related failure may be enormous. Perhaps the most revealing development is linguistic. Manufacturers now describe their systems with careful names that avoid the word autonomous, and require drivers to keep hands near the wheel. Whatever the technology can do, the language of liability is being designed separately, and it may end up shaping what is actually sold.",
      trans: "关于自动驾驶汽车的技术争论一直比法律争论容易。工程师可以就传感器范围与反应时间争论；而律师必须决定：当一辆车做出伤害某人的决定时，责任归谁。随着自动化程度提高，这个问题变得更尖锐。有人类驾驶员、且必须保持注意力的车在法律上很清楚：驾驶员负责。完全不需要注意力的车，则把责任转移给制造商——但也不完全，因为疏于保养的车主仍可能有过错。在这两极之间是最棘手的类别：部分自动化，即系统在驾驶，而人被期望在接到请求后数秒内介入。人因研究表明，这种安排可能最危险，因为当任务看起来不必要时，注意力衰减得非常快。监督自动系统二十分钟的驾驶员，面对突发要求时的反应远慢于他们自以为的速度。监管者的回应并不一致：一些司法辖区允许在配备安全驾驶员的情况下广泛测试；另一些允许在限定区域内进行完全无人驾驶，那里速度低、路线已测绘。与此同时，保险公司正在悄然改写假设，因为轻微碰撞的频率可能下降，而罕见的软件相关失效的代价可能极其巨大。也许最能说明问题的是语言上的变化：制造商如今用谨慎的命名来避免使用“自主”一词，并要求驾驶员把手留在方向盘附近。无论技术能做到什么，责任的语言正在被单独设计，而它最终可能决定实际卖出去的是什么。",
      words: [
        ["autonomous", "adj.", "自主的，无人驾驶的"], ["liability", "n.", "责任，法律责任"],
        ["intervene", "v.", "介入"], ["decay", "v.", "衰减"],
        ["supervise", "v.", "监督"], ["jurisdiction", "n.", "司法辖区"],
        ["neglect", "v.", "忽视，疏于"], ["revealing", "adj.", "发人深省的"]
      ],
      questions: [
        { q: "Which category does the passage call the most difficult legally?", options: ["Fully manual driving", "Partial automation where a human must intervene quickly", "Completely driverless cars", "Bicycles"], answer: 1 },
        { q: "Why may partial automation be the most dangerous?", options: ["Sensors fail more often", "Attention decays quickly when a task seems unnecessary", "Drivers are untrained", "Software is slow"], answer: 1 },
        { q: "What does the passage say about insurers?", options: ["They ignore automation", "They are rewriting assumptions about frequency and severity of claims", "They refuse to insure cars", "They set the speed limits"], answer: 1 }
      ]
